==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{proto|Germanic|wurþiz}}, from {{proto|Indo-European|wrti-}}, a verbal abstract from the root *{{term||wert-|to turn}} ( > Latin {{term|vertere}}), related to the Old English verb {{term|weorþan||to grow into, become}} (compare German {{term|werden}}).  Cognate with Old Saxon {{term|wurd}}, Old High German {{term|wurt}}, Old Norse {{term|urðr}} (Swedish {{term|öde}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/wyrd/}}

===Noun===
'''wyrd''' {{f}}

# [[fate]], [[destiny]]
#:''Gæð a wyrd swa hio scel.''  Fate always goes as she must.  (''Beowulf'' l. 455)
# {{in plural|lang=ang}} the [[Fates]]
# [[event]], [[occurrence]]

